  "response": "Attributing a system-wide collapse on a renewable-heavy network is an evidentiary exercise rather than a contest of narratives, because the operative causes are visible only in the first few seconds of the disturbance. The South Australian case is the reference point: a state-wide blackout occurred in South Australia on 28 September 2016, and the inquiry that followed had to decide between intermittency and protection design. Tornadoes during the storm damaged 23 transmission towers in the state's mid-north, and the blackout was triggered by a cloud-induced collapse in rooftop solar output; the felling of those towers and a cloud-driven loss of rooftop output cannot both have initiated the cascade, so one of the two accounts must give way.\n\nChronology supplies the first discriminator. After the towers came down, nine wind farms reduced their power output following a sequence of voltage disturbances on the network. That curtailment was not produced by any lull in the wind; it was caused by the turbines' own protection response. The wind turbines' fault ride-through protection activated once a preset number of voltage dips was exceeded, and that activation led to the loss of approximately 456 MW of wind generation within a few seconds.\n\nOn the interconnection to Victoria, the Heywood link tripped after being overloaded by the increased imports drawn across it. The loss of that interconnection in turn caused the South Australian region to separate electrically from the rest of the national electricity market during the event. Because of the separation, the rate of change of frequency exceeded the capability of the under-frequency load shedding scheme, so that no automatic defence could arrest the decline. Subsequently, restoration of supply to the majority of customers took five days.\n\nThe reconstruction of the sequence of events by means of time-synchronised dynamic simulation models of the power system was brought about by the investigators' collection of high-speed fault records and SCADA data from the affected generators. Only a reconstruction of that kind can distinguish a plant that ceased generating because the resource disappeared from one that ceased generating because its own settings instructed it to, and it is this distinction that decides the dispute.\n\nThe positions of the parties remain predictably opposed. The wind farm operators attributed the blackout to storm damage to transmission infrastructure, whereas the transmission network operator attributed the system collapse to the generators' protection settings. For operators and regulators alike, the resolution therefore lies in the recorded behaviour of the protection relays and in the measured inertia and frequency response of the islanded system, which are matters of record rather than of advocacy.",
